if you think of an emergency there's a  good chance this comes to mind  however for the 12 million deaf people  in the uk there's no siren to say help  is on its way and making that 999 call  isn't easy  but today a new service launches for the  thousands of deaf people who use british  sign language or bsl ambulance please  my dad's had a stroke one click of a  button on the app connects users to a  bsl interpreter via video link  through them they can talk to a 999 call  handler in real time  until now deaf people have had to ask  others to call for help or use a  text-based system  this will be faster but that's not all  the biggest difference it's going to  make is to give deaf people  the independence  to make those kind of calls themselves  um  in a language which they are most  comfortable in and when you're  distressed  you need to be able to communicate in  your own language and not be trying to  think of how to communicate in a  different language  because english actually isn't the first  language for many deaf people british  sign languages so imagine trying to call  an ambulance in french when you don't  speak it and then trying to get all the  medical information you need across as  quickly and accurately as possible  that's the situation bryony found  herself in when her husband suffered a  severe allergic reaction out on a run i  wasn't able to ring 999  and i thought about texting my mum to  ask her to call 999 but then i thought  that's going to take too much time and  then relaying the call  so i was really panicking my husband  couldn't breathe and he was in and out  of consciousness  bryony had to drive her husband to the  nearest hospital to seek help  happily he survived  this service could save others the  stress brownie went through and saved  lives  madeleine ratchcliff sky news
 

